      "id": "7p65nyk",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "PART A: BASIC CONCEPT OF SOIL"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "ICAR classifies Indian soils into 8 major categories based on origin, color, composition, and location.",
        "Alluvial soil is the most widespread and fertile soil, categorized into Khadar (new) and Bhangar (old).",
        "Black soil (Regur) has high clay content and water-retention capacity, making it self-ploughing and ideal for cotton.",
        "Laterite soil is formed due to intense leaching under conditions of high temperature and heavy rainfall.",
        "Soil conservation methods such as contour ploughing, terrace farming, and shelterbelts prevent soil erosion."
      ],
      "id": "fr69xkx"
    },
